{"artifact_digest":"sha256:2d7ceadc8b14ffad57f65ed06ae87466e6cdca2be460bf112e704cd2d5251948","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/2d/2d7ceadc8b14ffad57f65ed06ae87466e6cdca2be460bf112e704cd2d5251948.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:2d07524c659c32f4c74e0c1a1c8ac850ce6238d647cec56c396bdabba2ec9ee6","bundle_entry_key":"2609-02987-lemma-3-regularity-for-the-tailrl-grad","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_052","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_052.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-02987-lemma-3-regularity-for-the-tailrl-grad","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The proof verifies the three operations in order, at an arbitrary $\theta\in U$.

\par\noindent\emph{The score function identity.}
Fix a threshold $\tau$.
The tail-probability is the sum of the policy probabilities over the rollouts that clear it,
\begin{equation}
p_\theta(x,\tau)
=
\sum_z \mathbbm{1}_{\{r(x,z_j)>\tau\}}\,\pi_\theta(z\mid x).

\end{equation}
By (as:tailrl-smooth of the paper), every term of the differentiated series is bounded by the summable envelope of (eq:app-tailrl-smooth-policy of the paper), so the gradient of the sum is the sum of the gradients.

\begin{equation}
