[Lem of arXiv:2606.24385, 'Gradient Mean-Field Dynamics with Measure-Valued States: Well-Posedness, Chaos, and Long-T'] Let us suppose that (A4 of the paper)-(A5 of the paper), (A7 of the paper)-(A8 of the paper), and consider $y(t)=(x(t),\lambda(t))$ be a random variable with values in ${\mathscr Y}$ such that ${\mathbb E}\sup_{r\in [0,T]}\norma{y(r)}_{{\mathscr Y}}^{2}<+\infty$, and where $\lambda(r)$ follows the differential equation (equation eqperlambda of the paper). Then the integral form for (equation eqperlambda of the paper), namely, 

\begin{align}
\lambda(t)=\lambda(0)+\int_{0}^{t}{\mathbb R}(y(t),\Psi){\rm d} t + \int_{0}^{t}\Pi_{\lambda(s)}{\rm d} W^{Q}(s)
\end{align}
makes sense with respect to the seminorm ${\mathbb E}\norma{\cdot}_{{\rm BL}}^{2}$.

