[Proposition 8 (Fundamental estimate with localization parameter) of arXiv:2608.17540, 'Pathwise stability for one-dimensional SDEs driven by Brownian motion and a symmetric stab'] Assume Assumptions~\ref{ass:baseline}--\ref{ass:solutions}. Then there exists a constant
\[
C=C\bigl(T,\alpha,\wt\eta,\|\ell_b\|_{L^1(0,T)},\|\ell_a\|_{L^2(0,T)},\|\ell_\sigma\|_{L^\alpha(0,T)}\bigr)
\]
independent of $\delta$ and $\eps$ such that, for every $\delta\ge2$ and $\eps\in(0,1)$,
\begin{align}
\sup_{0\le t\le T}\E|X_t-\wt X_t|^{\alpha-1}
\le C\Bigg[&|x_0-\wt x_0|^{\alpha-1}
+\eps^{\alpha-1}
+\frac{\delta}{\log\delta}\frac{B}{\eps^{2-\alpha}} \notag\\
&+\frac{\delta^2}{\log\delta}\frac{A^2}{\eps^{3-\alpha}}
+\frac{\delta}{\log\delta}\frac{S^\alpha}{\eps}
+\frac{\delta^2}{\log\delta}\eps^{\alpha-1}
+\frac{\eps^{\alpha\wt\eta-1}}{\log\delta}
\Bigg]. 
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Th th_original_threshold of arXiv:2606.23648, '\textsc{Optimal Stopping for a Diffusion with Unobserved Bernoulli Drift}'] Assume that the hypotheses of Theorem~(th_2d_structure of the paper) hold. Then
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal C^0
        =
        \{x\in\mathcal I:\kappa(x)>0\}.
    \end{equation}
    In particular, for every \(x\notin\mathcal C^0\), immediate stopping is optimal and \(V(x)=k(x)\). For every \(x\in\mathcal C^0\), we have
    \begin{equation}
        V(x)=Q(x)G(0)+R(x),
    \end{equation}
    with the functions $Q$ and $R$ defined in (equation def_q_R_coefficients of the paper), and an optimal stopping time is
    \begin{equation}
        \tau_x^*
        =
        \inf\{t\ge0:X(t)\notin(x+a(x),\,x+b(x))\}.
    \end{equation}

    If, in addition, (ass_3 of the paper) holds, then the function $\kappa(\cdot)$ of (equation def_kappa_threshold of the paper) is non-decreasing on \(\mathcal I\), and \(\mathcal C^0\) is either empty or a right interval. More precisely, with
    \begin{equation}
        x^*
        \coloneqq
        \inf\{x\in\mathcal I:\kappa(x)>0\}
    \end{equation}
    and the convention \(\inf\emptyset=\infty\), immediate stopping is optimal for every \(0\le x<x^*\), while \(x\in\mathcal C^0\) holds for every \(x>x^*\).

    Finally, assume that \(J\subset\mathcal C^0\) is an open interval on which the relevant two-dimensional boundaries are of class \(C^1\). Then \(V\) is \(C^1\) on \(J\), with
    \begin{equation}
        V'(x)=Q'(x)G(0)+R'(x),
        \qquad x\in J.
    \end{equation}
    If \(x^*<\infty\), \(V(x^*)=k(x^*)\), and \((x^*,0)\) is a regular horizontal crossing point, then \(V\) satisfies smooth fit at the original threshold:
    \begin{equation}
        V'(x^*+)=k'(x^*).
    \end{equation}
    If \(x^*>0\), then \(V'(x^*-)=k'(x^*)\) holds as well, so \(V\) is continuously differentiable at
    \(x^*\).

