[Lem 75 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] Setting $\mathsf{t}' = \mathsf{t}/2$, we have w.o.p. that, for any $z \in \mathfrak{X}$ and $t \in [\mathsf{t}', N^5]$,
			\begin{flalign}

				H_t (z) \le \mathcal{H}_{q'} (z) + N^{\delta-1}, \quad \text{if either $q-q' \le \mathtt{d}_z^{-1/3} N^{A\delta-2/3}$ or $\mathtt{d}_z^{1/2} \mathtt{e}_{z;q'}^{3/2} \ge N^{4A\delta-1}$.}
			\end{flalign}

[Thm 49 of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Let $d \geq 1$ and consider the setting of Assumption \ref{ass:SRI}.
For any $t\ge 0$, and any $\phi\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^d)$, we have that 
\begin{multline}\lim_{N\to\infty} N^{\frac{d-2}2} \sum_{r=0}^{Nt} \Ebbnn \big[\phi(N^{-1/2} (   R^1_r -    s_N r)\big)f_N(   R^1_r-   R^2_r) ] \\ =\bigg( \int_0^t \int_{\mathbb R^d} G(2s,x_1-x_2) G(2s,y) \phi(\tfrac12(y+x_1+x_2)) \dr y\dr s\bigg) c_d \int_I f \;\dr\pi^{\mathrm{inv}}
\end{multline}
whenever $N^{-1/2}\mathbf x_N \to \boldsymbol x= (   x_1,   x_2)\in \mathbb R^{2d},$ where $   x_{1}\ne    x_{2}$. Here $G$ appearing on the right side is the standard heat kernel on $\mathbb R^d$, that is $G(t,y) = (2\pi t)^{-d/2} e^{-|y|^2/(2t)}.$ Furthermore, $c_d$ and $\pi^{\mathrm{inv}}$ are as in Definition \ref{def:unit}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

Let (X, Sigma) be a measurable space, let lambda be a finite nonnegative measure on Sigma, and let (mu_n) be a sequence of countably additive finite signed (or complex) measures on Sigma with each mu_n absolutely continuous with respect to lambda. Suppose the limit mu(E) = lim_n mu_n(E) exists in the scalars for every E in Sigma. Then the family (mu_n) is UNIFORMLY absolutely continuous with respect to lambda: for every eps > 0 there is delta > 0 such that lambda(E) < delta implies |mu_n(E)| < eps for every n. Consequently (Nikodym's theorem) the set function mu is itself countably additive and absolutely continuous with respect to lambda.
Countable additivity of every mu_n;
a single finite dominating lambda;
convergence of mu_n(E) for EVERY measurable E, not merely on a generating algebra.

[Definition def:spec-Borel of arXiv:2606.21391, 'Extreme points and faces in the moment problem'] Let $\mu$ be a Borel measure on a topological space $X$. Then $\mu$ is a 
\emph{Radon measure}

\footnote{Radon measures are also known as \emph{tight} measures [topsoe70].}

[schwartz73] if $\mu$ is inner regular on compact sets, that is to say, the 
measure $\mu(A)$ of any $A\in\mathcal{B}(X)$ is the supremum of $\mu(B)$ over all compact 
subsets $B\subset A$. The measure $\mu$ is \emph{$\tau$-smooth}

\footnote{It is well known that every Radon measure is $\tau$-smooth
[bogachev07-vol2]. Also, for every $\tau$-smooth 
measure $\mu$ there exists a smallest closed set $A$ of full measure 
$\mu(A)=\mu(X)$, called the \emph{support} of $\mu$.}

if $\mu(\bigcup_{i\in I}A_i)=\sup_{i\in I}\mu(A_i)$ for every increasing net 
$(A_i)_{i\in I}$ of open sets [topsoe70,winkler85]. Let $R$ and $P\!_\tau$
be the sets of Radon and $\tau$-smooth probability measures on $X$, respectively.

[Lemma lem:Poisson of arXiv:2606.08325, 'Digit Mixing under Polynomial Maps'] For every fixed \(K\ge1\), the following convergence in distribution holds:
			\begin{equation}
			(A_{N+1},\ldots,A_{N+K})
			\xrightarrow{D}
			(Z_1,\ldots,Z_K),
			\end{equation}
			where \(Z_1,\ldots,Z_K\) are independent Poisson random variables of mean $\lambda := \frac{C^d}{d!}\Gamma(1/d)^d$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

