    Theorem (thm:critical of the paper) states exactly that
    \begin{equation*}
        X_N \xrightarrow[N\to \infty]{\mathbb{P}} c_{\theta},
    \end{equation*}
    where $c_{\theta} = e^{(\theta - 2)\gamma} > 0$. Since $\tau_{N, \theta}^{(1)} > 0$ almost surely, we have $X_N > 0$ almost surely, so the logarithm is well defined. Because $\log$ is continuous on $(0, \infty)$, the continuous mapping theorem gives
    \begin{equation*}
        \log X_N \xrightarrow[N \to \infty]{\mathbb{P}} \log c_{\theta} = (\theta - 2)\gamma.
    \end{equation*}
    Hence,
    ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:fc375c5f036bb212bf81b1590484d8ec0620026e4a29120bf00c451d78a3f338","revision_digest":"sha256:e5778f1032ad3e51c801dff54b38ac662d128fdf4cf60ae88a3361ab22c76df8","source_id":"arxiv:2607.08551","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

