{"artifact_digest":"sha256:e72d45e06543f928895a04e48a1620e7e4625ef41533e7c5496275247d9d5585","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/e7/e72d45e06543f928895a04e48a1620e7e4625ef41533e7c5496275247d9d5585.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:c3c7e9e01719d7f8d7abb32e34ee2b1410144f51830ca203b89bf8aaac517cd0","bundle_entry_key":"2602-07920-theorem-5-leigenvector","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_065","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_065.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2602-07920-proposition-5-leigenvector","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Note that $L$ is a lower triangular matrix and $L(i, i)=2^{-i}$. In particular, the set of eigenvalues of $L$ is $\{2^{-1-j}: 0\leq j\leq n-1\}$. Fix $0\leq j\leq n-1$ and note that 
    \begin{align*}
        (Lv^{(j)})_{i} = 2^{-i}\sum_{k=1}^{i}\binom{i-1}{k-1}(k-1)^{\underline{j}}=2^{-1}\;\mathbb{E}[X^{\underline{j}}]=2^{-j-1}(i-1)^{\underline{j}}\;,
    \end{align*}
    where $X$ is a Binomial random variable $X\sim \mathrm{Bin}(i-1, 1/2)$. And the last equality follows from the well-known identity for the factorial moment of a Binomial random variable
    \[
    \mathbb{E}\left[\mathrm{Bin}(n, p)^{\underline{m}}\right] = p^{m}n^{\underline{m}}\;.
