{"artifact_digest":"sha256:5917d2e6b394e32d89635bbfaa7af8331a2a1c7864f00804a7c794f983b7fb81","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/59/5917d2e6b394e32d89635bbfaa7af8331a2a1c7864f00804a7c794f983b7fb81.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:783d3913de8ee92192c5c653e49b8c8f59556957e1250edd41a43e347f0a7cc4","bundle_entry_key":"2506-15736-lemma-24-upperboundminrate","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s2_005","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s2_005.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2506-15736-lemma-24-upperboundminrate","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The proof of this lemma is essentially the same as the proof of Lemma 4.6 in \cite{Johnston2023}. We can first see that if $x_1 + \cdots + x_N = x$ then there exists $j$ such that $x_j \geq x/N$. Noting that $\psi_v$ is an increasing function for all $v \in V$, we have 

    \begin{align*}
        \Omega(x) = \min_{x_1 + \cdots + x_N = x} \sum_{i = 1}^N \psi_{v_i}(x_i) \geq \min_{j = 1, \ldots, N} \psi_{v_j}(x/N).
    \end{align*}

    \noindent We thus can obtain the following upper bound

    \begin{align*}
        \frac{1}{\Omega(x)} \leq \frac{1}{\min_{j = 1, \ldots, N} \psi_{v_j}(x/N)} = \max_{j = 1,\ldots, N} \frac{1}{\psi_{v_j}(x/N)}\leq \sum_{i = 1}^N ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:9e1e3c70ff7c012fe40db9cbff5e19da4043a7f53195a2750545eaadff61ee80","revision_digest":"sha256:ea54ff4165a3037b894a85ac5d0915ae472581a709e2537bd0f25701d980e9f4","source_id":"arxiv:2506.15736","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

