[Lemma lm:T.bds of arXiv:2411.02046, 'Speed of Convergence and Moderate Deviations of FPP on Random Geometric Graphs'] Let $d \geq 2$ and $r>r_c$. Then, there exist $C,c>0$ and $\beta^\dagger >1$ such that, for all $x \in \R^d$ and every $t> \beta^\dagger \|x\|$,
    \begin{equation} 
        \mu\big(D(o,x) \geq t\big)  \leq Ce^{-c t}. 
    \end{equation}

    Additionally, if (equation A2 of the paper) holds, then there exist $c_{1}, c_{2}>0$ and $\beta>1$ such that, for all $x \in \R^d$ and every $t> \beta \|x\|$,
    \begin{equation} 
        \p(T(x)\geq t) \leq c_{1} e^{-c_{2} t}, 
    \end{equation}

[Proposition prop:diffusionType of arXiv:2412.05030, 'Characterization of Subordinate Symmetric Markov Processes'] ([BKKL])

  Assume $(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})$ satisfies the diffusion type heat kernel estimate for a scale function $\phi_c$ and 
  let $S$~ be an independent subordinator whose Bernstein function is given in ((eq:Bernstein function of the paper))
  with $\phi := \phi_c$.
  Then, the jump kernel $J(x,y)$ of the subordinate process satisfies 
  the following estimate: 
  \begin{equation}
    J(x,y) \simeq \frac{1}{V(x,r)} \frac{1}{\psi(r)},
  \end{equation}
  where $r := d(x,y)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

