body:
[Theorem thm:local_clt_intro of arXiv:2507.10531, 'Quantitative central limit theorems for exponential random graphs'] Define
\begin{equation}

    \varsigma_n^2 = \mleft( 1 - p(1-p) \sum_{j=1}^K \beta_j p^{\mathsf{e}_j-2} \sum_{\rho \in \mathcal{V}_j} \mathsf{d}_\rho (\mathsf{d}_\rho-1) \mright)^{-1} \times p (1-p) (n-1).
\end{equation}
Then for any deterministic vertex $v \in [n]$ and any $\varepsilon > 0$, we have
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{d} \mleft( \frac{\mathsf{deg}_v(X) - \mathbb{E}[\mathsf{deg}_v(X)]}{\varsigma_n}, Z \mright) \lesssim n^{-\frac{1}{4}+\varepsilon},
\end{equation}
for both $\mathbf{d} = \mathbf{d}_\mathrm{Was}$ and $\mathbf{d} = \mathbf{d}_\mathrm{Kol}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

