body:
[Thm 4 of arXiv:2608.26642, '2608.26642'] Let $f\in C(\R)$. The following assertions are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item There exists a constant $C$ such that the function $f$ satisfies the Fredholm equation
\begin{align}

f(x)
=C+\int_K f(y)h(x-y)\,\mu(dy)\qquad \text{for}\ x\in \R,
\end{align}
with the normalization condition
\begin{align}

  \mu[f]=1.
\end{align}
\item The function $f$ satisfies the growth condition
\begin{align}

f-h\in C_b(\R)
\end{align}
(which implies $f\in \mathscr{A}_0$) and the Sturm--Liouville equation
\begin{align}

\mathscr{L}f
=f\cdot \mu,
\end{align}
that is,
\begin{align}
\langle \mathscr{L} f,g\rangle =\int_\R g(x) f(x)\,\mu(dx)\qquad \text{for}\ g\in C_c^\infty(\R).
\end{align}
\end{enumerate}
If either, and hence both, of these conditions holds, then we have $f=\varphi^\mu.$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:weak-one-edge-estimates of arXiv:2608.23135, 'Graph distance and effective resistance of the random walk trace in four and five dimensio'] For every fixed $R\ge2$, as $n\to \infty$, 
\begin{equation}

        \max_{e\in\mathcal P_\vartheta,\,\ell(e)\le R}
        \left|\frac{q_e}{p_e}-1\right|\longrightarrow0 .
\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}

        \sum_{e\in\mathcal P_\vartheta}q_e+
        \sum_{e\in\mathcal P_\vartheta}p_e\le C_\vartheta,
\end{equation}
and, for all $R\ge2$,
\begin{equation}

        \sum_{\substack{e\in\mathcal P_\vartheta\\ \ell(e)>R}}q_e+
        \sum_{\substack{e\in\mathcal P_\vartheta\\ \ell(e)>R}}p_e
        \le \frac{C_\vartheta}{R}+o_n(1).
\end{equation}

