body:
[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2608.19179, 'A random walk on $p$-groups with a symmetric perfect pairing'] Suppose $1 \leq k \leq n$. If $M_{k,k}$ is non-singular, then there is a canonical isomorphism of groups equipped with a symmetric pairing:
\begin{equation}

V_k^{\perp} \Big/ V_k \cong \Ker(M_{k,k}) \times \Q_p^{n-k} 
\end{equation}
where the pairing on the right hand side is the sum of the pairing on $\Ker(M_{k,k})$, and some symmetric pairing on $\Q_p^{n-k}$. The pairing on the left hand side is the pairing induced by $M_{n,n}$.

[Theorem thm:rectangular Appell polynomials of arXiv:2605.31356, 'P\'olya--Schur problems and free probability'] Let $\{f_{n}\}\subset\mathcal{LPI}$ and satisfy Assumption (assump:main assumption of the paper). If $\beta\equiv\beta_n$ and $\frac{n}{n+\beta}\rightarrow \lambda\in (0,1]$, then \begin{equation}
		\left\llbracket f_n\left(\frac{\lambda}{n}\mathscr{L}_\beta\right)z^{n}\right\rrbracket\Rightarrow\mathsf{MP}_{1,\lambda}\boxtimes \mu_{c,\Sigma},
	\end{equation} 
	 where $\mu_{c,\Sigma}$ is the $\boxplus$-ID limit of the Appell polynomials $A_{n,g_n}$ from Theorem (thm:main result, general of the paper) and $\mathsf{MP}_{1,\lambda}$ is the standard Marchenko--Pastur distribution with density 
	\begin{align*}
		\mathrm{d}\mathsf{MP}_{1,\lambda}(x)
		=\frac 1 {2\pi\lambda x} \sqrt{\big((1+\sqrt{\lambda})^2-x\big) \big(x-(1-\sqrt{\lambda}\big)^2  }\big)\mathrm{d} x \text{ on } x\in[(1-\sqrt{\lambda})^2, (1+\sqrt{\lambda})^2].
	\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop mb:mvsde of arXiv:2411.11759, 'Milstein-type schemes for McKean--Vlasov SDEs driven by Brownian motion and Poisson random'] If Assumptions \mbox{\normalfont  (asum:ic of the paper)} to  \mbox{\normalfont (asum:con of the paper)}   are satisfied, then there exists a unique solution of  the McKean--Vlasov SDE (equation eq:sde of the paper) as well as of its associated interacting particle system (equation eq:int of the paper). 
		Further, when Assumption \mbox{\normalfont (asum:lin** of the paper)} also holds,  {then there exists a constant $K>0$ such that, for all $N\in \mathbb N$}
		\begin{align*}
		\sup_{t\in[0,T]}E|x_t|^{\bar p}\leq K \mbox{ and }\sup_{i\in\{1,\ldots,N\}}\sup_{t\in[0,T]}E|x_t^{i,N}|^{\bar p}\leq K.
		\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 3 (Lower bound for H\"older functions) of arXiv:2608.31157, 'Sharp Approximation Rates for Neural Networks with Affine Latent Parameterizations'] Let $d\in\N^+$ and $0<\alpha\le1$.  There exists a constant
$\underline{c}_{\alpha,d}>0$ such that, for every $M,P\in\N^+$,
\begin{equation}

 \calE_{\alpha,d}(M,P)
 \ge
 \underline{c}_{\alpha,d}
 [P\min\{M,P\}]^{-\alpha/d}.
\end{equation}
The constant is independent of $M$, $P$, the network depth, the architecture,
and the affine map. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 23 of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] Every normalized Horn pair has an asymptotic realization.  In
particular, if
\begin{equation}
 \Gamma(K,L)>1-2\delta,

\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
 R_D(\delta)\leq\Phi(K,L).

\end{equation}

[Proposition 20 of arXiv:2604.05234, 'Quantitative propagation of chaos and universality for asymmetric Langevin spin glass dyna'] Let $\widehat X$ be a strong solution of \eqref{eq: fully regularized SDE}. Then $\widehat X\in \mathbb L^{1,2}$, and in particular for every $i=1,\dots,N$
\begin{align*}
    \int_0^T \int_0^T \mathbb{E}[|D_s^i \widehat X_t|^2]\,ds\,dt\leq C,
\end{align*}
where $C$ does not depend on $N, \frs$ or $h$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma shifting RW of arXiv:2411.14350, 'Randomized Geodesic Flow on Hyperbolic Groups'] Let $(X_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ be a random walk on a hyperbolic group $G$ with symmetric step distribution $\mu$ whose support is finite and generates $G$ and starting point $X_0=o$. Fix $m\in \mathbb{N}$ and set
\begin{align*}
\bar Z^m_n &:= X_m^{-1}X_{m-n} &&\text{ for } n=0,...,m;\\
Z^m_n &:= X_m^{-1}X_{m+n} &&\text{ for } n\geq m\\
\end{align*}
Then the following hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\big(\bar Z^m_n\big)_{n=0}^m$ and $\big(Z^m_n\big)_{i=0}^{\infty}$ are independent for any $m\in\mathbb{N}$. 
\item Furthermore, they converge in distribution to independent random walks $\big(\bar Z_n\big)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ and $\big(Z_n\big)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ with step distribution $\mu$ and starting point $o$.
\item Finally, $\big(\bar Z_n\big)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ and $\big(Z_n\big)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ converge a.s to points $Z_{\infty}$ and $Z_{-\infty}$ on $\partial G$.
\end{itemize}

